General elections were held in [[Malawi]] on 15 June 1999 to elect the [[President of Malawi|President]] and [[National Assembly of Malawi|National Assembly]]. They were originally scheduled for 25 May,<ref>[http://www.eisa.org.za/wep/mal1999bg2.htm Malawi: 1999 Elections (1998-1999)] EISA</ref> but were postponed twice as a result of requests by the opposition to extend the voter registration period.<ref name=IPU/> Both votes were won by the ruling [[United Democratic Front (Malawi)|United Democratic Front]], who took 93 of the 192 seats in the National Assembly, and whose candidate, [[Bakili Muluzi]], won the presidential election with an absolute majority.

In total, eleven parties contested the elections, with 670 candidates.<ref name=IPU>[http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/arc/2195_99.htm Malawi: Parliamentary Chamber: National Assembly: Elections held in 1999] Inter-Parliamentary Union</ref> Voter turnout was 94%.<ref>[http://www.eisa.org.za/wep/mal1999results2.htm Malawi: 1999 Presidential election results] EISA</ref>

